92186093198 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1061 37829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2236e99f-a53e-57c2-91ab-c3091e8a86a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1061 37830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b4c7081-0037-5d22-8c22-bf18957c01d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1061 4197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90c92812-0369-53f2-8139-3a602c9d8ddf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1061 4198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11713b46-54cf-58b5-8dc5-2984d413ed5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1061 4201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd94fe81-ec77-53b8-8ef8-e461ac344d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1061 4204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cabcbccd-aabb-5b9e-a421-ce222693f535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1064 "When we add, subtract, or multiply two integers, the answer is always an integer. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two integers:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b=2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na + b = 3+2 = 5\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na - b = 3-2 = 1\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na \\cdot b = 3\\cdot 2 = 6\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- However, dividing the two integers does *not* give an integer:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac32 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol\\times}}\n$$\n\nWe say the integers are **closed** under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but **not closed** under division.\n\n**Watch Out!** Although integers are not closed under division, the ratio of two integers is *sometimes* an integer. For example, if $a=4$ and $b=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} =  \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2,\n$$\n\nwhich is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1065 1593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7d0a253-b58a-5205-b05d-d5d2f1d49f6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093431 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider once again the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square that is spanned by these vectors.\n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/8f/8fca8cf6dd5b232d3aed6eeb98fb6c244872c55425673cf3ac1441a22e2e29f3.svg) \n\nAs before, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/3f/3f8b5f0f827d359f32a4311021dc48947447133918d48a2f1cad511531b13c81.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/41/4195c8cd2f22fb0f84056226fb7b28c5f9944f747ca3230bf187752829b2a04e.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, *clockwise* about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093431 #edb/ref 1065 3542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093431 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "70a75b2e-3fa9-5aa9-b308-79b56d57fae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093431 #edb/ref 1067 "Rotations of 90 Degrees" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093432 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/c6/c6865c5373231451885b3d76e3c8151e9c707462a8b405d9b014b1bda17065a8.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n![](images/5b/5b8e1c8e03ebb60716eaa2fefded5e7fc286f6de9cffdece8200f6f56c2bbe8e.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c2dc9f76bb23225cec0a4f62541d8a74c4c4b16d2e2f7c37e8d36922d97c59f0.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) about the origin.**\n\nNote that we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) *clockwise* about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093432 #edb/ref 1065 3545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093432 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "410fc9b7-ab2d-52ba-9b26-7afd34fdf9e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093432 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093434 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, let \n\n![](images/1b/1b840cbdb5b3953dfef9910d34248ea280bf38299e4f0a3d0a78143e7301f159.svg)\n\nbe a matrix of a linear transformation ![](images/2d/2d90be63023ff97e21e1a97ccee6c387437ac2a99787278c6f71f37e3ac86850.svg) As usual, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/76/76e24611a2cd8a3d5d01fc0a04a9b7a3d0957db84dd1a45bc2f1f82492fb2efd.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0f/0fc5f33d0bd7047ab02bd9bc3ea3b00c083ab214f3bc8a51e9cbb6e54dc6b51b.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians *clockwise* about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093434 #edb/ref 1065 3547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093434 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "49541094-17cb-52e7-bb58-8ee2199ad6d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093434 #edb/ref 1067 "Rotations of 270 Degrees" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2x^{\\color{blue}4} - 3x + 1, \\qquad\nQ(x) = -6x^{\\color{red}3} + 5x^2 + x\n$$\n\nNotice that these polynomials have degrees $4$ and $3,$ respectively. We write this as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) = {\\color{blue}4}, \\qquad\n\\textrm{deg}(Q) = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, \n\n- the leading term in $P(x)$ is $2x^{\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the leading term in $Q(x)$ is $-6x^{\\color{red}3}$\n\nAs a result, the leading term in the product $P \\cdot Q$ equals the product of the leading terms of $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n2x^{\\color{blue}4} \\cdot (-6x^{\\color{red}3}) = -12x^{{\\color{blue}4}+{\\color{red}3}} = -12x^{\\color{purple}7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be ${\\color{purple}{7}}.$\n\nWe have the following general rule:\n\n> *The degree of the product of two polynomials equals the sum of the degrees of these polynomials:* \n> \n> $$\n> \\textrm{deg}(P \\cdot Q) = \\textrm{deg}(P) + \\textrm{deg}(Q)\n> $$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1065 6201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60187490-7cd6-566d-ab45-128e2c4f81c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1067 "The Degree of a Product of Two Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now consider the degree of the sum and difference of two polynomials $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nFirst, let's suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have *different* degrees. In this case, the degree of the sum or difference equals the largest of the two degrees. We write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) = \\max \\big\\{ \\textrm{deg}(P), \\textrm{deg}(Q) \\big\\},\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $\\textrm{max}$ function simply returns the largest element of the set.\n\nFor example, suppose that $\\textrm{deg}(P) = 5$ and $\\textrm{deg}(Q) = 2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P + Q) = \\max \\big\\{ 5, 2 \\big\\} = 5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why this is true, note that the leading term of $P$ contains an $x^5,$ and the leading term of $Q$ has an $x^2.$ When we add the two polynomials, no term of $Q$ could cancel out the leading term of $P.$ So the sum of the polynomials must have degree $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1065 6202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fae91d47-b786-5c2f-9a29-dfb06624930f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1067 "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1064 "Polynomials are also closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but not closed under division.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = x+1, \\qquad Q(x) = x^2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =(x+1)+({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-Q & =(x+1)-({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\cdot Q & =(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{2}+1\\cdot {x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- However, *dividing* the two polynomials does *not* give a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{P}{Q} & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** Although polynomials are not closed under division, the ratio of two polynomials is *sometimes* a polynomial. For example, if $P=x^2$ and $Q=x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P}{Q} =  \\dfrac{x^2}{x} = x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1065 6228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d529e51a-742c-52f5-874b-546cf98a1849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1067 "Closure Properties of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1064 "Now suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have the *same* degree. That is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) =   \\textrm{deg}(Q) = n.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this case, the degree of the sum or difference can be *any integer* from $0$ to $n.$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) \\leq n\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Let $P$ and $Q$ be polynomials of degree $15.$ Then, the *largest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $15$ since the resulting polynomial can't contain terms of order higher than $15.$\n\n- On the other hand, the *smallest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $0.$ For instance, suppose $P=x^{15}$ and $Q=1-x^{15}.$ Then $P +Q = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1065 6229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f9d9fe12-e873-5b5b-aca8-f212f9be62b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1067 "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096665 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096665 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096665 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096665 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096665 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096665 #edb/ref