688 #edb/ref 1061 16238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101688 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1acb842-d646-50c3-b138-c3d25f4aec2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101688 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101689 #edb/ref 1061 16239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a915d8c2-6eb6-5077-9e2e-597348800c3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101689 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101690 #edb/ref 1061 16240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ddbb79fe-cd5e-51a7-a873-1eb6188a8325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101691 #edb/ref 1061 16241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "222ac5de-6fe2-55b8-a6e5-18988da71e89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101700 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101700 #edb/ref 1061 17254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101700 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba53472f-59c9-53a7-bc81-a3233068d585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101700 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101963 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 1, 3 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 2,-1 \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/10/102f72ba6730d1b07ca0d01186a1ff7851954193f00dc74eb7150981844b0222.png)\n\nNow, we construct a new matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Let's find the determinant of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|det(M)| \\\\  & =|-7| \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101963 #edb/ref 1065 3014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101963 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90a7caf1-54f7-517e-93cb-98a6d39494ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101963 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101964 #edb/ref 1064 "Why does the absolute value of the determinant represent the area of the parallelogram? Let's prove this result.\n\nFirst, let's state the result precisely. Consider two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle a,b \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle c,d \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/3a/3a466fd3c52ea345d80b9ba67ac104efef29bfd7eeed0e4567364c80f3212c0d.png)\n\nWe construct a new matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Then the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA = |\\det(M)|\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we use the following picture:\n\n![](images/3d/3da110b114d2038738bd4ef06cb031bb8b66aee062ffc83955dfdaade72584e1.png)\n\nWe can compute the area by starting with the area of the outer rectangle, and then subtracting off the area of the smaller triangles and rectangles surrounding the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|(a+c)(b+d)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}cd-2\\cdot ad-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}ab| \\\\  & =|ab+ad+bc+cd-cd-2ad-ab| \\\\  & =|bc-ad| \\\\  & =|ad-bc| \\\\  & =|det(M)|\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101964 #edb/ref 1065 3015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101964 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca25b302-10d5-5d18-a10f-0c50af28db94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101964 #edb/ref 1067 "General Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101966 #edb/ref 1064 "When two vectors are parallel, the parallelogram does not exist. So, it has zero area.\n\n![](images/b6/b65c64ccf16764f99c0c30dbf6b37ce7b6254b83496512a5d207dddab095dac7.png)\n\nAs a result, the determinant of a matrix whose columns are parallel vectors must also be zero. \n\nFor example, in the diagram above, we have the vectors $\\textbf{u} = \\left< 1,1 \\right>$ and $\\textbf{v} = \\left< 2,2 \\right>.$ Constructing a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIndeed, the determinant does come out to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101966 #edb/ref 1065 3173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101966 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ff830819-9cbf-5fb9-852c-f885f8cd6d68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101966 #edb/ref 1067 "The Case When Two Vectors are Parallel" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186153896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153892 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5ce1db30-6521-52ee-b054-913135f1b843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153893 #edb/ref 1080 "A" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153893 #edb/ref 